<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.ScopeBar"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False">

    <!--
        WHICH LIST THIS IS - services, drivers, or both. Owner's decision, 2026-08-19, and it is the
        control `docs/11` recorded as "waiting on the owner" and FilterChips.cs named as "an IA
        change rather than a grouping. Named, not smuggled."

        ABOVE THE SEARCH BOX AND NOT BESIDE THE FILTERS, because it is not one of them. The filters
        row narrows the list somebody is looking at. This decides WHICH LIST that is, and the
        question underneath is then asked inside it. Standing it among the chips would say the
        opposite of what it does, which is the mistake the drivers chip was making for a week.

        ACROSS THE TOP RATHER THAN DOWN THE LEFT, and that was measured against first party guidance
        rather than chosen. Microsoft recommends left navigation for five to ten equally important
        categories, and where switching is NOT expected to be frequent - its stated cost is "less
        space for other app content". Top navigation is recommended for five or fewer, and when you
        want more room for content. There are three positions here, an administrator comparing a
        service with the driver under it switches constantly, and this window's oldest complaint -
        number 5 of the eleven - is about wasting horizontal space. Owner's decision, 2026-08-19,
        after asking for the left and reading the guidance.

        RadioButton RATHER THAN ToggleButton, AND IT IS ACCESSIBILITY RATHER THAN TASTE. Three
        toggles would need the window to keep them exclusive by hand, and a screen reader would meet
        three unrelated on-off buttons instead of one choice of three. A RadioButton carries
        SelectionItem in the automation tree and moves under the arrow keys inside its group for
        free. `docs/10` trap 7 is the same lesson from the other end: a window can look perfect and
        be invisible to a reader.

        Focusable and IsTabStop are off because a UserControl IS a ContentControl and a
        ContentControl is BOTH BY DEFAULT. Without them this row is a tab stop that does nothing in
        front of the positions it holds. `docs/10` trap 8.
    -->
    <!--
        AN ItemsControl RATHER THAN THREE CONTROLS WRITTEN OUT, exactly as the filters row does it.
        Which positions there are, in which order, and why each one is kept is decided in Scopes.cs
        beside the reason for it - markup listing them again would be a second copy that drifts.

        THE AutomationId IS ON THE ItemsControl AND NOT ON A PANEL, which this window has now paid
        for five times: WPF builds an automation peer for controls, not for layout panels, so an
        identifier on a StackPanel sits on an element that is not in the tree at all.
    -->
    <!--
        A DockPanel SO THAT NEITHER CHILD CARRIES A NUMBERED POSITION, and the order below is the
        order of the Tab key: the positions first, then GitHub, Donate and Help, then the search box
        under this row.
        The positions keep the left, and the last child takes what is left of the width and stands
        at its right end.
    -->
    <DockPanel>
        <ItemsControl DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="scopeSwitch"
                      AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource gui.scope.hint}"
                      ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.scope.hint}"
                      ItemsSource="{Binding ScopePositions}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>

            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate>
                    <!--
                        TwoWay, and the false half is thrown away on purpose - the argument is at
                        ScopeChoice.IsOn. A radio group tells the OUTGOING position it is unchecked
                        before it tells the incoming one it is checked, and acting on that would mean
                        writing a scope on the way out of one.
                    -->
                    <!--
                        AND A PRESS IS HEARD EVEN ON THE MARKED TAB, since 2026-10-20 (T-e). The tabs stay
                        over the overview and the views of the audit, with the list's tab marked, and a
                        radio button already checked changes nothing when pressed - so the press is what
                        takes somebody back to the list from there. ScopeBar.xaml.cs forwards it.
                    -->
                    <RadioButton Style="{StaticResource ScopePosition}"
                                 Content="{Binding Label}"
                                 Tag="{Binding Count}"
                                 ToolTip="{Binding Hint}"
                                 IsChecked="{Binding IsOn, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                 Click="PositionPressed" />
                </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>

        <!--
            THE LINE BETWEEN THE LISTS AND THE TOOLS, since 2026-10-20 - owner's decision T-a: the three tabs
            before it choose which list, and the one after it is a page of its own. The action bar's rule,
            by name. Its margin matches the gap every tab leaves after itself, so the line stands in the
            middle of the space rather than against the Tools tab.
        -->
        <Rectangle DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                   Style="{StaticResource BarRule}"
                   Margin="{StaticResource MarginGroup}" />

        <!--
            THE TOOLS TAB. The same style and the same group as the three before it, so a screen reader meets
            one choice of four pages and the look cannot drift from theirs. Nothing in Tag, so no number.
            Its mark ignores false for the reason ScopeChoice.IsOn does (Tabs.ToolsTabOn).
        -->
        <RadioButton x:Name="ToolsTabButton"
                     DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                     Style="{StaticResource ScopePosition}"
                     AutomationProperties.AutomationId="toolsTab"
                     Content="{DynamicResource gui.tools.tab}"
                     ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.tools.tab.hint}"
                     IsChecked="{Binding Tabs.ToolsTabOn, Mode=TwoWay}" />

        <!--
            THE ONE THING THIS WINDOW ASKS OF ANYBODY, since 2026-09-23 - owner's decision, at the
            right end of the top row. It stands here because this row has room to its right and is
            always on screen, and because it is the row furthest from the work: the list, the
            filters and the actions all sit below it. The right end lines up with Columns, the last
            control of the filters row, because neither carries a right margin.

            THE LIBRARY'S OWN BUTTON, exactly as Columns and Show every instance wear it - the same
            family of control, with nothing to make it louder than they are. The heart and the word
            are the pattern of the Filters toggle, including the name set above the content, which
            stopped being a string.

            The tooltip says what pressing it does BEFORE it is pressed, and says that this program
            still connects to nothing - the one moment a person could feel ADR-19 was broken is the
            moment a browser appears. Nothing about starting anything is here: the click is wired
            in MainWindow, and ExternalLinks is the file allowed to hand an address over.
        -->
        <!--
            AND HELP AT THE VERY END SINCE 2026-09-24 - UX-GUI-014, owner's decision. The corner is
            where people look for help, so Help has it and Donate stands just left of it. One panel
            for the two so the Tab key meets them in the order the eye does. Help carries no right
            margin, so it is Help that lines up with Columns now.
        -->
        <StackPanel HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Center" Orientation="Horizontal">
            <!--
                GITHUB SINCE 2026-10-14, owner's decision - the project's repository, LEFT of Donate so
                that Donate and Help stay where people already find them. Text only, the way Help is:
                the heart is Donate's because it is the one thing this window asks of anybody, and a
                second mark beside it would make it one of two. The press is wired in MainWindow and
                the address is a constant in ExternalLinks, exactly as Donate's is.
            -->
            <Button x:Name="GitHubButton"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="github"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource gui.repository.button}"
                    ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.repository.hint}"
                    Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                    Content="{DynamicResource gui.repository.button}" />

            <Button x:Name="DonateButton"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="donate"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource gui.support.button}"
                    ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.support.hint}"
                    Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}">
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                    <Path Style="{StaticResource SupportMark}" />

                    <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource gui.support.button}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </StackPanel>
            </Button>

            <!-- The menu is hung on it by MainWindow.Help.cs, the items are HelpMenu.cs. -->
            <Button x:Name="HelpButton"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="help"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource gui.help.button}"
                    ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.help.hint}"
                    Content="{DynamicResource gui.help.button}" />
        </StackPanel>
    </DockPanel>
</UserControl>
